The T1-weighted images were skull stripped using the FreeSurfer 5.3 auto-recon pipeline 56 and reoriented to the standard Oxford Centre for the Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging of the Brain Software Library (FSL) orientation using fslreorient2std 57 . The linear registration was performed using flirt 58 , which utilized linear interpolation and the default 1 mm FSL template (version 6.0). The borders were cropped in [6:173, 2:214, 0:160] coordinates to minimize size while retaining the complete volume. Last, the voxel intensity values were normalized to the range of [0,1], adjusting the intensity values of each voxel to a standardized scale.

In our study, we utilized the ACAPULCO version 0.2.1 algorithm 29 , which is a state-of-the-art cerebellum parcellation algorithm based on convolutional neural networks. This algorithm is part of the ENIGMA Cerebellum Volumetric Pipeline version 3.1 and provides speedy and accurate quantitative in vivo regional assessment of the cerebellum at the highest fidelity 59 . We used pre-processed T1-weighted images for better quality control and alignment. The inhomogeneity of the images was corrected using the N4 60 and registered to the 1 mm isotropic ICBM 2009c template in MNI space using the ANTs registration suite 61 . The ACAPULCO algorithm was trained using 15 expert manual delineations of an adult cohort 59 . It performs per-voxel labeling of the cerebellum and applies post-processing to remove isolated pieces, ensuring accurate segmentation. The algorithm divides the cerebellum into 28 cerebellar lobules, including bilateral lobules I-VI; crus I and II; lobules VIIB, VIIIA, VIIIB, and IX-X; vermis VI, VII, VIII, IX, and X; and corpus medullare. It also calculates the volume (mm 3 ) of each region. The automated quality control generates segmented images for each participant, and we removed the extreme outliers where the number of lobules exceeded a certain threshold (for example, n &gt; 2).

## Voxel-level processing

In our study, we utilized the SUIT version 3.4 (Spatially Unbiased Infratentorial Toolbox) 62 toolbox to perform segmentation of cerebellar gray and white matter voxel-based morphometry maps. This segmentation process involved using the outputs from ACAPULCO, which include the N4 bias-corrected and MNI-aligned T1 image 62,63 , as well as an averaged mask derived from 300 randomly selected individuals without diagnosis. The ACAPULCO mask plays a crucial role in correcting and refining overinclusion errors in the segmentation process due to variations in the segmentation algorithm. Following segmentation, the gray matter maps were normalized and resliced to align with a standardized space. This normalization step ensures that the data can be compared across different individuals and studies. In addition, the gray matter maps were modulated by the Jacobian to preserve the value of each voxel in proportion to its original volume. This modulation accounts for individual differences in brain size and helps to retain the relative intensity values within the mapped brain regions. By using the SUIT toolbox and incorporating the ACAPULCO outputs, we were able to obtain accurate and spatially unbiased segmentation of cerebellar gray and white matter, enabling further analysis and comparison of voxel-based morphometry maps within and across individuals.

## Normative modeling

We split our sample of individuals without diagnosis into training and test sets on the basis of on scanning site, sex, and age. This split is important to account for the potential confounding effects of MRI scanners on the data 17,64,65 . The individuals without diagnosis were first stratified on the basis of the scanning sites. This ensures that the training and testing datasets include a representative distribution of participants from each location. To achieve this, we evenly split the control participants from each scanning site between the training and testing sets. A minimum of five participants from the same scanning site was required. However, only the test set consisted of diagnostic groups (for example, AD, ASD, BD) with a minimum of 100 participants. This criterion ensures that there is an adequate number of participants in each diagnostic group to provide reliable statistical analyses. By employing this stratification approach, we aimed to create balanced and representative training and testing datasets that account for MRI scanners and sex, while also including enough participants in each diagnostic group.

We used the PCNtoolkit package (version 0.24) 15,66 in Python 3.8 to estimate a normative model for predicting regional cerebellar volumes and voxel-wise intensity on the basis of sex and age, while correcting for scanning site. Results that deviated more than five standards of deviation were imputed by the mean. We employed a Bayesian linear regression with likelihood warping approach 67 , specifically using the 'sinarcsinsh' transformation 65,68 . This approach is well suited for handling nonlinear basis functions and non-Gaussian predictive distributions for large datasets as well as correcting for outer centiles. A detailed mathematical description on Bayesian linear regression for normative modeling can be found in ref. 65. To account for scanner effects, we treated the scanning site as a fixed effect in our analysis 16,69 . This approach has been shown to yield relatively good performance, as demonstrated in previous work 16 . To assess how each participant ( i ) deviates from the individuals-without-diagnosis pattern at each lobule or voxel ( j ) in the cerebellum, we calculated the z score:

<!-- formula-not-decoded -->

The computation of the z score includes predicted mean ˆ yij (lobule or voxel), true response yij , predicted variance σij and normative variance σnj . For model fit, the normative model provided point estimates and evaluation metrics, including explained variance, mean squared logloss, skew, and kurtosis 68 . These evaluation metrics were computed in the test set that did not include any clinical groups. To determine participants with extreme deviations, we set a threshold at z &gt; |1.96|, corresponding to the 95% confidence interval. For example, deviations with z scores greater than 1.96 were identified as extreme positive deviations, indicating significantly increased volume compared with the control pattern and vice versa for extreme negative deviations.

