63:The normative modeling approach allows us to quantify extreme positive and negative deviations, here defined as |z| &gt; 1.96, at the voxel-wise and lobular levels for each individual. Figure 2b,c shows examples of normative probability maps for different individuals across clinical diagnostic groups. These maps reveal high cerebellar heterogeneity within and across groups, especially when we look at the overlaps of these individual-level probability maps. The overlap maps calculate the percentage of extreme deviations within the same group for each voxel. Even within the same diagnostic group, only a marginal percentage of individuals show extreme deviations in the same cerebellar regions. Moreover, some cerebellar regions (for example, in posterior lobules) show increased numbers of extreme deviations across several diagnostic groups (for example, SZ, ASD, and AD). Analyses of lobular volumes yielded similar results (Supplementary Fig. 4b,c).
67:Voxel-wise normative models can be projected onto any existing, or future, cerebellar atlas morphed into Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) space (Fig. 3a). In this Article, the voxel-wise normative probability maps are summed across 28 lobules (anatomical atlas), 10 functionally defined cerebellar regions (task-based atlas) 28 , and 17 regions defined by their resting-state connectivity with functional networks of the cerebral cortex (resting-state atlas) 29,30 . The percentage of deviations for each region is calculated across parcellations for each individual. As the percentage of negative deviations increases, the strength of the clinical groups also increases. SZ, MCI, and AD groups showed small to medium effects in the negative deviations compared with the individuals without diagnosis globally across all three atlases. For all three groups, the effects are visibly greater in the posterior regions of the cerebellum and display consistent patterns across the atlases. However, ASD and MCI groups reveal significant small effects in positive deviations across numerous regions. For ASD, the medium effects in the
77:Figure 4 shows correlations obtained from three distinct atlases when examining the relationship between estimated intelligence scores. In ASD, regions associated with right IV-VI in the anatomical atlas and somatomotor B (network 4) in the resting-state atlas showed positive correlations between percentage of extreme positive deviations per participant and estimated performance IQ (performance intelligence quotient, PIQ) (Supplementary Tables 7-9) that survived multiple comparisons of number of tests, regions of interest, and directions. Weak positive correlations between the percentage of extreme positive deviations per participant and estimated intelligence scores were shown in patients with SZ across three atlases and vice versa for negative deviations (Fig. 4a and Supplementary Tables 13-15). However, symptom scores of the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS) for ASD and Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale for SZ did not show significant associations with percentage of extreme deviations per participant after correcting for multiple comparisons (Supplementary Tables 10-12 and 16-18).
