81:Leveraging brain MRI data from &gt;54,000 individuals, we chart cerebellar lifespan trajectories with voxel-wise precision using normative modeling. These models are estimated across 132 scanning sites and contribute to the field by (1) providing approximately 143,000 normative models of the cerebellar lobules and voxels across the lifespan, (2) confirming and extending previous reports of altered cerebellar structure in various mental and neurological illnesses, (3) demonstrating considerable structural cerebellar heterogeneity within all clinical groups, (4) coupling normative models with existing cerebellar atlases to enhance interpretability, and (5) demonstrating functional significance of extreme deviations in terms of associations with measures
127:While our work represents one of the largest investigations into the cerebellar structures and heterogeneity at the level of the individual, and provides a resource for researchers for future investigations, some limitations require consideration. First, we lack coherent and detailed behavioral, cognitive, genetic, phenotypic, and medical history information for both individuals without diagnosis and clinical samples. This is usually the case when combining information across multiple datasets from different projects as documentation and assessment vary, which may preclude certain associations from being tested. In addition, the coverages of lifespan normative models in the very young age and in the age range of 30-40 years were relatively low. We tested for robustness in the supplementary analyses through comparison with an age-, sex-, and scanning-site-matched control group. Since the sample size of the matched comparison is smaller, the threshold for statistical significance is also affected. We hence report both corrected and uncorrected results. Due to limited availability regarding ethnicity information, this paper represents primarily western populations, in particular, European groups, which limits its generalizability. In the future, norms for different ethnicities should be established. This paper focuses on large-baseline cross-sectional data, which limits the examination of the effects of drugs or treatment. Moreover, the local thematically organized psychosis (TOP) study has incomplete information regarding medication status and duration of medication use and exposure, hindering further examination. Because of its location in the posterior fossa, its intricate arrangement, and motions artifacts, the cerebellum has posed challenges in imaging studies, and therefore inferior regions of the cerebellum may result in poorer model fits. The cerebellar topography shows substantial individual differences 51 and therefore does not perfectly align with existing 'average' atlases. Therefore, there are limitations to the interpretation of functional implications. These results should be interpreted with caution as the IQ estimates used here are based on a wide range of tests used across groups. The IQ measures used here are mere estimates of intelligence and not an accurate reflection of intelligence, which is arbitrarily defined in the first place 52 . Moreover, the terminologies of PIQ and VIQ from ABIDE-I and ABIDE-II are outdated as the newest version of the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale uses the Verbal Comprehension Index Scale and Perceptual Reasoning Index scale. As correlation does not imply causation, we further do not want to imply a causeand-effect relationship between cerebellar deviations and cognitive function. Nonetheless, and if interpreted with caution, we believe that the analysis of cognitive function and estimated IQ provides further meaningful evidence as to the complex role of the cerebellum across different disorders and diseases. The resource established here can be used in future studies to further elucidate the complex etiology of mental and neurological illnesses. By including comprehensive longitudinal datasets or dividing the heterogeneous clinical population into subtypes, we can aim to enhance biomarker development using cognitive and behavioral measures 21,53-55 .
129:We report the largest multi-site investigation of heterochronic development and aging of the cerebellum at both voxel-wise and lobular spatial precision. Through normative modeling, we observe individualized patterns of deviation across five different mental and neurological illnesses. Several clinical phenotypes exhibited negative deviations at the group level, but with notable individual differences even within the same clinical groups. Overall, this study charts cerebellar morphology across the lifespan, provides evidence for the differential involvement of the cerebellum across brain illness, and links extreme deviations from population norms to cognitive function.
